EXERCISE 2_1 — OUTPUT INTERPRETATION
====================================

WINDOW "Original"
-----------------
Code:   Mat src = imread("../dark_flower.bmp", IMREAD_COLOR);
        imshow("Original", src);
Result: Raw BGR color image. No processing applied.
        Yellow petals, dark background, visible greenery.

WINDOW "Blue"
-------------
Code:   split(src, channels);
        imshow("Blue", channels[0]);
Result: Dark image. Yellow petals are nearly black.
Why:    Yellow = high R + high G + low B.
        channels[0] = Blue. Blue values are low -> dark pixels.

WINDOW "Green"
--------------
Code:   imshow("Green", channels[1]);
Result: Bright petals, dark background.
Why:    Yellow contains high green -> bright.
        channels[1] = Green.

WINDOW "Red"
------------
Code:   imshow("Red", channels[2]);
Result: Brightest petals of the three.
Why:    Yellow leans strongly red -> high pixel values.
        channels[2] = Red.

WINDOW "Hue"
------------
Code:   cvtColor(src, hsv, COLOR_BGR2HSV);
        split(hsv, hsvCh);
        imshow("Hue", hsvCh[0]);
Result: Mostly dark / low contrast.
Why:    Hue encodes COLOR TYPE, not brightness.
        Flowers are all similar yellow hue -> narrow value range.
        Dark = low hue value (yellow sits low in 0..179).

WINDOW "Saturation"
-------------------
Code:   imshow("Saturation", hsvCh[1]);
Result: Very high contrast. Petals white, background black.
Why:    Saturation = purity / amount of color.
        Pure yellow petals -> high S -> bright.
        Gray background -> low S -> dark.

WINDOW "Value"
--------------
Code:   imshow("Value", hsvCh[2]);
Result: Looks like a normal grayscale photo.
Why:    Value = brightness (max of R,G,B).
        Essentially the grayscale version of the image.

====================================================
CODE -> OUTPUT MAP (one line each)
====================================================

imread + imshow("Original")       -> raw BGR photo
split -> channels[0] -> "Blue"    -> dark petals
split -> channels[1] -> "Green"   -> bright petals
split -> channels[2] -> "Red"     -> brightest petals
cvtColor(BGR2HSV) -> hsvCh[0]     -> low-contrast hue map
cvtColor(BGR2HSV) -> hsvCh[1]     -> high-contrast purity map
cvtColor(BGR2HSV) -> hsvCh[2]     -> grayscale-like brightness